      "page_end": 87,
      "text": "Jeff Rice describes this last point well when he states, \"The best demonstration of choral moves on the Web can be seen (but not only found) in the hypertextual link that allows writers the capability of developing threads around single words or ideas, and that requires readers to navigate these threads in various ways. The link is indicative of a new media push to reorganize space in terms of meaning construction\" (Rhetoric 35). Note Rice's observation that choric invention involves more than just finding, since it attends to a reorganization of space, meaning that invention is integrated into the process of finding itself; there is, then, no simple \"finding\" exterior to the ambient media environment. And as Vitanza remarks, such new media writing, or electracy, as Ulmer often calls it, is choric in that it too is a third kind, following but being neither progress from nor a hybrid of orality and literacy (\"Writing\" n.p.). In other words, there is no need to reach to a Hegelian narrative of sublation to understand choric writing. Indeed, it might be better to say that rhetorical invention has always been choric, that topic writing was simply the way choric writing has predominately been disclosed to us, and that new technologies have afforded us fresh perspectives on invention that resonate strongly with the ch6ra. Topic writing is not abandoned, then; it is inhabited differently. But this also means that choric invention is itself ubiquitous; a book, a web text, a classroom, and a viral video making the Internet rounds would all have their choric bearing.\n\nIf choric writing is ubiquitous-ambient, as it were-then the stakes in adopting it lie not in demonstrating the \"reality\" of what is in fact everyday activity but in garnering the ability to bring it to rhetorical theory or finding\n\nin such practices fresh rhetorical opportunities. In this respect, the chora allows Ulmer to reconceive the relations between a writer and his or her \"specific position in the time and space of a culture\" (Heuretics 33). This is not solely a matter of the hermeneutics of cultural identity, except insofar as they are extended to a broader project of generating texts appropriate for the electronic age and the question of how an inventio suited to such generation can be formulated. Hawk describes this as using \"the function of location to map your specific position in time, place, and culture and invent a heuretic in the process\" (Counter-History 240). Ulmer's chora moves us from thinking that is \"linear indexical\" to a form that is \"network associational\" (Heuretics 36) and is concerned less with logic than with memory (experience, both personal and externalized/stored) and intuition (37), less with verification than with learning (xii). The radical expansion and externalization of memory in cultural discourses, electronic networks, and databases creates an ocean of information that in turn requires navigation (30). One must be careful on this point, however. As suggested previously by both Kristeva and Derrida, the chora is precariously placed in regard to reasoning and discourse, meaning that it stretches our prior and rather entrenched sense of what these things might be. Ulmer observes that by providing new equipment of memory, computers also transform people and institutions (36). Choric navigation, then, moves us toward a reconceptualization or a fresh disclosure of place accompanied by a further refinement of what it means to navigate, associate, and compose. For Ulmer, this will mean, among other things, that a hypermedia composer constructs not arguments per se but an \"information environment\" through which a user will choose a path. Such a notion of place, and the kinds of activities that emerge from it, are properly understood as dispersed or distributed. As Rice's previously cited observation indicates, this is precisely why the hyper link has such inventive capacity, since it enacts distribution.\n\nBut given the centrality of place in these discussions, I want to further emphasize that these rhetorical forms rely on processes of externalization and dispersion. Encoding word and image in the electronic media involves an externalization, to be sure, and Ulmer takes time to develop these ideas in detail. But such processes accentuate an ongoing attunement to the materialities of body, place, and environment that have been picked up in numerous disciplines at this time, including the sciences. Such attention to materiality has helped produce a sense of dispersion for the human subject that extends the insights of French poststructuralist thought. Katherine Hayles, for example, has written about this dispersion in the ongoing work",
